//! check: fail(E0206)
//! phase: lex
//! conforms: gram.type.start, gram.type
//!
//! s163 (wolf-lang#320) — a keyword where a type must begin is E0206,
//! "expected a type", at the keyword ([gram.type.start]). `proc` is a
//! keyword (it spells `spawn proc`), not a type, so the parameter
//! position of this `fn` type holds a token that cannot begin one. The
//! compiler has answered E0206 since about bs12 and no corpus file
//! pinned it, so an implementation reading only the spec and the corpus
//! had nothing to transcribe (lupin once spent the number on an
//! invention of its own). Both machines answer E0206 at the `proc`
//! since lupin 0.1.32's is45. #320's own spelling, `fn f(p: proc)`, is
//! refused the same way; it is not the witness because the formatter
//! cannot fully format a signature refusal under a `//!` header
//! (wolf-lang#396), and every corpus file must.

fn main() -> !int {
    let g: fn(proc) = 0
    0
}
